An elegant and optimal solution in PHP for **Abbreviating the Product of a Range**.

### Method Explanation

1. **Count Trailing Zeros ($C$):**
   The number of trailing zeros in the product is determined by $\min(\text{factors of } 2, \text{factors of } 5)$. We count the factors of 2 and 5 contributed by each number in the range $[left, right]$ and set $C = \min(cnt_2, cnt_5)$.

2. **Last Digits & Threshold Check:**
   We iterate through the range again, dividing out $C$ factors of 2 and $C$ factors of 5 from the numbers. The product of these reduced numbers is identical to the original product after removing all trailing zeros.
   - We accumulate the product in `$suf`. If at any point `$suf \ge 10^{10}$, we know the total number of digits $d > 10$. In this case, we set a flag `$over = true` and reduce `$suf \pmod{10^5}$` to track only the last 5 digits.
   - If `$over` remains `false`, `$suf$ directly holds the exact remaining product ($d \le 10$), which can be returned as `"$suf" . "e$C"`.

3. **First 5 Digits:**
   If $d > 10$, we compute the first 5 digits using logarithms. Since trailing zeros (factors of 10) only shift the decimal point without changing the most significant digits, the leading digits of the original product and the product without trailing zeros are identical.
   We compute $S = \sum_{x=left}^{right} \log_{10}(x)$ and extract the fractional part $f = S - \lfloor S \rfloor$. Then $\lfloor 10^f \times 10^4 \rfloor$ yields the first 5 digits.

### PHP Source Code

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer $left
     * @param Integer $right
     * @return String
     */
    function abbreviateProduct($left, $right) {
        $cnt2 = 0;
        $cnt5 = 0;

        for ($i = $left; $i <= $right; $i++) {
            $x = $i;
            while ($x % 2 == 0) {
                $cnt2++;
                $x /= 2;
            }
            while ($x % 5 == 0) {
                $cnt5++;
                $x /= 5;
            }
        }

        $c = min($cnt2, $cnt5);

        $c2 = $c;
        $c5 = $c;
        $suf = 1;
        $over = false;

        for ($i = $left; $i <= $right; $i++) {
            $x = $i;
            while ($c2 > 0 && $x % 2 == 0) {
                $x /= 2;
                $c2--;
            }
            while ($c5 > 0 && $x % 5 == 0) {
                $x /= 5;
                $c5--;
            }

            $suf *= $x;
            if ($suf >= 10000000000) {
                $over = true;
                $suf %= 100000;
            } elseif ($over) {
                $suf %= 100000;
            }
        }

        if (!$over) {
            return $suf . "e" . $c;
        }

        $sum_log = 0.0;
        for ($i = $left; $i <= $right; $i++) {
            $sum_log += log10($i);
        }

        $diff = $sum_log - floor($sum_log);
        $first5 = (int)(pow(10, $diff) * 10000 + 1e-11);
        while ($first5 >= 100000) {
            $first5 = (int)($first5 / 10);
        }

        $last5 = sprintf("%05d", $suf % 100000);

        return $first5 . "..." . $last5 . "e" . $c;
    }
}
