An optimal and elegant way to solve this problem is using a greedy approach. 

### Method Explanation
If the largest battery has a capacity greater than $\lfloor \frac{\text{sum}}{n} \rfloor$, that battery has more charge than the maximum possible simultaneous running time for all computers. Therefore, it can be dedicated exclusively to run one computer for the entire duration. We can then remove this battery, decrement the number of computers $n$ by $1$, reduce the total sum by this battery's capacity, and repeat the process.

Once all remaining batteries have a capacity less than or equal to $\lfloor \frac{\text{sum}}{n} \rfloor$, none of them will have wasted capacity, and the answer is simply $\lfloor \frac{\text{sum}}{n} \rfloor$.

